# trust-matrix identity matrix — one identity per line, '|'-separated:
#   label | kind | auth | cookie
#
# kind must be one of: owner, acct, anon, expired, revoked
#   owner   = the control; fetches ITS OWN object (must return 200). Exactly one required.
#   acct    = a second, separate account (the attacker identity).
#   anon    = no credentials. If this leg also gets the data, it's public -> auto-downgrade.
#   expired = a token whose lifetime has passed (tests expiry enforcement).
#   revoked = a token you explicitly revoked / logged out server-side (tests session invalidation).
#
# You supply the sessions. trust-matrix never creates accounts (AUP) and only sends GET.
# Leave the auth and cookie fields empty for anon. Either an auth header or a cookie works.

TenantB          | owner   | Bearer <OWNER_TOKEN>    |
TenantA          | acct    | Bearer <OTHER_TOKEN>    |
anon             | anon    |                         |
TenantB-expired  | expired | Bearer <EXPIRED_TOKEN>  |
TenantB-revoked  | revoked | Bearer <REVOKED_TOKEN>  |
